{"results":[{"url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/grand-island-maintains-property-tax-rate-despite-county-increases","final_url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/grand-island-maintains-property-tax-rate-despite-county-increases","title":"Grand Island maintains property tax rate despite county increases","description":"Grand Island's property tax rate will remain unchanged after the city council voted to lower the mill levy for the seventh consecutive year. The budget was offi","language":"en","text":"# Grand Island maintains property tax rate despite county increases\n\n---\n\nby Ariana Joy Cobler\n\nWed, September 10, 2025 at 3:26 PM\n\nGrand Island Property Tax Revenue History\n\nGRAND ISLAND, Neb. — Grand Island's property tax rate will remain unchanged after the city council voted to lower the mill levy for the seventh consecutive year. The budget was officially approved last night with a 9-1 vote.\n\nAccording to the Internal Revenue Service mill levies are adjusted periodically to meet local budget needs, usually with public input or approval. A higher mill rate means higher property taxes. Lower property tax rates can increase demand for properties in an area.\n\nWhile Hall County may be raising property taxes, the city of Grand Island has kept its property tax collections steady at approximately $12 million since 2018. This is in contrast to other Tri-Cities, where tax collections have risen by about $1.5 million since 2017.\n\nThe newly approved budget includes $24 million for parks and recreation projects. According to the city council, $16 million of tax revenue will be invested in Island Oasis, with the remainder allocated to refreshing and renewing parks throughout Grand Island.\n\n\"They want to attract more people to the city - and he says one way to do that is with the parks,\" said City Council Member Jason Conley.","author":"Ariana Joy Cobler","published_date":"2025-09-10T20:26:08.000Z","latency_ms":1451.392572002078,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}